cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Understudy: converting Bally Squawk & Talk TMS5200 speech ROMs
  for TMS5220-family replacements
message: >-
  If you use Understudy in research or preservation work, please cite it.
type: software
authors:
  - name: Flashback Fleet LLC
    website: "https://github.com/flippin-balls"
repository-code: "https://github.com/flippin-balls/understudy"
abstract: >-
  The Texas Instruments TMS5200 speech processor used on the Bally
  Squawk & Talk sound board is long out of production. The TMS5220,
  TMS5220C and TSP5220C share its frame grammar and field widths but
  use different LPC coefficient tables, so existing speech data parses
  correctly on them and sounds wrong. Understudy re-indexes the speech
  data in a ROM into the substitute part's tables. Because no field
  changes width the conversion is length-preserving, so a ROM can be
  patched in place with no pointer table, phrase boundary or timing
  altered. The tool reports what it cannot fix: the TMS5220 family
  cannot reach the TMS5200's lowest pitches, and those frames are
  raised.
keywords:
  - TMS5200
  - TMS5220
  - TSP5220C
  - linear predictive coding
  - speech synthesis
  - pinball
  - Bally
  - Squawk and Talk
  - retrocomputing
  - digital preservation
license: 0BSD
version: 0.3.0
date-released: "2026-09-06"
references:
  - type: software
    title: MAME
    notes: >-
      Source of the bundled TMS5200 and TMS5220 coefficient tables,
      from src/devices/sound/tms5110r.hxx, used under BSD-3-Clause.
      The tables are decap-verified upstream.
    authors:
